<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<ModMetaData>
  <name>The Shattered Crown</name>
  <author>cfrolik</author>
  <packageId>fr0.theshatteredcrown</packageId>
  <!-- The mod's own version, independent of the RimWorld version it targets.
       IgnoreIfNoMatchingField keeps older RimWorld builds (which have no
       such field) from erroring on it. Bump with:
           scripts\Build-Release.ps1 -SetVersion 1.1.0
       which keeps this and the assembly version in step. -->
  <modVersion IgnoreIfNoMatchingField="True">1.0.6</modVersion>
  <supportedVersions>
    <li>1.6</li>
  </supportedVersions>
  <modDependencies>
    <li>
      <packageId>brrainz.harmony</packageId>
      <displayName>Harmony</displayName>
      <steamWorkshopUrl>steam://url/CommunityFilePage/2009463077</steamWorkshopUrl>
      <downloadUrl>https://github.com/pardeike/HarmonyRimWorld/releases/latest</downloadUrl>
    </li>
    <li>
      <packageId>Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey</packageId>
      <displayName>RimWorld - Odyssey</displayName>
      <downloadUrl>https://store.steampowered.com/dlc/294100/</downloadUrl>
    </li>
  </modDependencies>
  <loadAfter>
    <li>brrainz.harmony</li>
    <li>Ludeon.RimWorld.Royalty</li>
    <li>Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey</li>
    <!-- Big overhauls first, content after: the conventional shape, and it
         keeps their patches applying to Core rather than racing ours.
         Nothing here is required - this mod names no def from either of
         them and detects their content at runtime instead. -->
    <li>DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul</li>
  </loadAfter>
  <!-- Combat Extended must come AFTER this mod, and the reason is concrete:
       CE patches other people's content. Its Patches folder and its gun and
       apparel auto-patchers only see defs that are already loaded, and this
       mod ships its own weapons and armor (the Kingsblade above all).
       Loaded the wrong way round, CE never sees them: the Kingsblade keeps
       vanilla Tools, which in CE's penetration model means no armor
       penetration at all, and the campaign's one irreplaceable weapon
       cannot trouble a man in plate. -->
  <loadBefore>
    <li>CETeam.CombatExtended</li>
  </loadBefore>
  <!-- Royalty is NOT required: it is only in loadAfter so the optional
       MayRequire brazier in the village prefabs resolves when it is present.
       Every ability icon is this mod's own art, and the psycast-flavored
       defs we use (PsychicShock, PsycastAreaEffect, Ability_Duration) all
       live in Core. -->
  <!-- Plain text: RimWorld's mod list does not render BBCode. The rich
       Steam page version lives in About/WorkshopDescription.txt and is
       pasted onto the item page by hand (RimWorld only sends a description
       when it first CREATES the workshop item, never on updates). -->
  <description>A medieval fantasy party-based RPG. You do not build a colony: you control a handful of companions, take work from the Wayfarers' Guild, and hunt the five pieces of a dead king's crown.

Features:
- Companions who are written characters
- 50,000 words of dialogue, with options gated behind skill checks that show their odds
- A d10 proficiency system across eleven skills, rolled in conversation and out in the world
- Eleven classes and over 40 abilities, multiclassing supported, powered by a new resource (Energy) that regenerates during sleep
- Optional turn-based tactical combat: initiative order, action points
- Multi-level dungeons you descend by finding the stairs
- Two scenarios: the authored campaign, or Adventure Mode's procedural contracts

Requires the Odyssey expansion (openable containers, tile mutators, and underground structures). All other expansions are optional.

Credits: spell icons by Lorc (https://lorcblog.blogspot.com) and gear icons by Delapouite (https://delapouite.com), both CC BY 3.0, via game-icons.net (Lorc's also in the 700+ RPG Icons pack on OpenGameArt).</description>
</ModMetaData>
